                <pb facs="0467" n="391" sameAs="frus:frus1935v02/pg391"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">862.4061 Merry Widow/1: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Secretary of State to
                        the Chargé in Germany</hi> (<hi rendition="#italic"
                            >White</hi>)</head>
                <opener>
                    <dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Washington</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1935-01-24T18:00:00-05:00">January 24, 1935—6
                        p.m.</date></dateline>
                </opener>
                <p>5. Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer state their film “The Merry Widow” has been rejected by
                    the German censor and that no reason was given, although usually the German
                    censorship authorities give one. The Company is at a loss to understand this
                    decision, in view of the unexceptionable character of the production and its
                    unusual musical merit. It has no desire to question the censor’s right of
                    rejection, but is interested, if possible, in learning the reasons for such
                    action with a view to the possible remedying of the difficulty.</p>
                <p>Please make discreet inquiry into this matter and submit a telegraphic
                    report.</p>
                <p>Frederick Strengholt of the Berlin Office of Metro-Goldwyn-Mayer is fully
                    informed regarding the case and may perhaps be consulted with advantage.</p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">Hull</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
